WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:10.080
The Gulf War ended 30 years ago, and the Iraq&nbsp;
War ended a decade ago, yet the Congressional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:15.040
authorizations for both wars remain on the&nbsp;
books. It's welcome news that Congress is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:20.320
looking to repeal these outdated measures. However&nbsp;
some, like Bernie Sanders, want to go further and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:25.200
propose a new War Powers Act that assumes that&nbsp;
only Congress has the authority to send American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:30.720
troops into combat. This Congressional overreach&nbsp;
threatens the Constitution's architecture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:35.760
which envisioned an executive capable of acting&nbsp;
decisively to protect the country, its citizens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:41.200
and its rights under international law. It's&nbsp;
no surprise that as American security concerns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:46.960
and obligations have grown, so to the president's&nbsp;
writ. But it is also Congress's job to push back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:52.480
against presidents who now claim a right to engage&nbsp;
in hostilities to protect some undefined notion of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:57.840
national interest. There is no more important&nbsp;
decision than putting US troops in harm's way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:03.280
Hence, fully understanding each branch's unique&nbsp;
role in that decision is no less important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:10.640
today than it was in 1787. Should Congress&nbsp;
amend the War Powers Act? And if so, how?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:15.280
Let us know in our poll. Also, let us know what&nbsp;
other topics you'd like our scholars to cover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:20.720
in 60 seconds, and be sure to like and&nbsp;
subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

